From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:35:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: fix the chroma offset of the full range YUV to RGB
 matrices

The FMT matrices that convert full range YCbCr surfaces to RGB treat
chroma code 2^(n-1) - 1 as zero chroma (127 for 8 bpc, 511 for 10 bpc,
2047 for 12 bpc) instead of 2^(n-1), which BT.601, BT.709, BT.2020 and
JPEG all use, and which the limited range matrices in the same table
already use. Every pixel gets a small chroma bias: mid gray from a full
range 8 bpc surface comes out with red about 2.5 and blue about 2.8 codes
higher than green, a slight magenta tint; 0.6 codes at 10 bpc.

Recompute the offset column of the nine full range entries for a chroma
zero of 2^(n-1). The coefficients don't change. The hardware reads a
component as code / 2^n (the full range luma coefficient is 2^n / (2^n -
1)), so the offset of each row is -(Cr coefficient + Cb coefficient) / 2,
rounded the way the limited range entries are; recomputing those with the
same formula reproduces them exactly.

This affects YUV planes whose color range is full: the legacy DRM
COLOR_RANGE / COLOR_ENCODING properties and NVKMS clients that request
NVKMS_INPUT_COLOR_RANGE_FULL. Clients using the DRM color pipeline program
the matrix themselves.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
